        <p>
          Most party games share a hidden flaw: someone has to run them. Someone
          explains the rules, someone keeps score, someone decides when it's
          over — and the moment that person gets distracted, the game stalls. A
          spin wheel doesn't have that problem. It sets its own pace, it needs
          no explanation, and it hands the "who goes next" decision to something
          nobody in the room controls.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's really the whole appeal. Load a list of prizes, dares, or
          questions, hit spin, and the room's attention snaps to the screen for
          fifteen seconds of genuine suspense. Repeat as many times as the party
          needs. No setup between rounds, no rules to re-explain, no points
          system to track.
        </p>
        <p>
