        <!-- ═══ SECTION 3 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="birthday-dares">3. Birthday Dare Wheels by Age Group</h2>
        <div class="party-label">🎂 Birthday Party</div>

        <p>
          A dare wheel isn't a prize wheel in reverse — it asks something rather
          than gives something, and what counts as funny rather than
          uncomfortable shifts enormously by age. Below are four dare wheels,
          each calibrated to a different crowd.
        </p>

        <div class="idea-grid">
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🧒</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Dare Wheel 01 · Kids (Ages 5–12)</div>
            <h3>Silly Challenge Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Keep it physical, quick, and never embarrassing. Solid options:
              hop on one foot for 30 seconds, do your best animal impression,
              recite the alphabet backwards, spin five times and try to walk
              straight, make the birthday person laugh without touching them, or
              invent a secret handshake on the spot with the person next to you.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card purple">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🎓</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Dare Wheel 02 · Teens (Ages 13–17)</div>
            <h3>Social Challenge Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Lean into social fun without pushing into genuine awkwardness.
              Good options: text the last person you messaged "you're awesome,"
              do a 30-second stand-up bit about the birthday person, swap shoes
              with someone nearby for 10 minutes, say something genuinely kind
              about everyone in the room, or show off your most random hidden
              talent.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card teal">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🥂</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Dare Wheel 03 · Adults</div>
            <h3>Party Forfeit Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              For adult groups who already know each other well: sing the
              opening line of any song a cappella, deliver a 60-second toast for
              the birthday person, trade one item of clothing with your
              neighbour for 15 minutes, speak only in questions for five
              minutes, or do an impression of someone in the room and let them
              guess who.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card gold">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🌈</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Dare Wheel 04 · Mixed Ages</div>
            <h3>All-Ages Safe Challenge Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              For a room spanning toddlers to grandparents, keep every segment
              achievable and warm: sketch the birthday person in 60 seconds,
              lead a 30-second stretch or dance, show how you used to dance as a
              teenager, teach the room one word in another language, or share
              the funniest thing that happened to you this year.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 4 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="birthday-trivia">4. "How Well Do You Know Me?" Wheel</h2>
        <div class="party-label">🎂 Birthday Party</div>

        <p>
          This is usually the format guests remember longest. It uses answers
          collected from the birthday person in advance and challenges the room
          to guess them — part trivia, part gentle roast, part love letter, and
          it works for nearly every age group.
        </p>

        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Collect the birthday person's answers beforehand</strong>
              <p>
                A week or so before the party, get the birthday person to
                privately answer 10–15 questions about themselves. Reliable
                ones: What's your most embarrassing childhood memory? What was
                your first job? What song would be your walk-on music? What
                would you do with a windfall of money? Who was your childhood
                crush? What's your most irrational fear?
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Turn each question into a short wheel segment</strong>
              <p>
                Keep the on-wheel label short and legible — "Embarrassing
                memory?", "First job?", "Walk-on song?", "Biggest fear?" — then
                read the full question aloud when it lands.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Let guests shout guesses before the reveal</strong>
              <p>
                Spin, read the full question aloud, give the room 20 seconds to
                guess, then have the birthday person reveal the real answer. You
                can track points for who gets closest, or skip scoring entirely
                for a looser, more relaxed feel — both work well.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Crown a "Best Guesser" at the end</strong>
              <p>
                Whoever scored highest gets a small prize or the honour of
                giving the closing toast. It's a natural, earned moment that
                doesn't require anyone to have prepared a speech in advance.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="box box-pink">
          <div class="box-label">🌸 Add a "roast" segment for extra warmth</div>
          <p>
            Slip in one or two "🎤 Roast!" segments. When it lands, whoever
            spoke most recently gives a short, affectionate-but-teasing line
            about the birthday person. Set the tone up front — toast, not burn —
            and it tends to produce the most-remembered moments of the whole
            party.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 5 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="baby-shower">5. Baby Shower Wheel Ideas</h2>
        <div class="party-label purple">🍼 Baby Shower</div>

        <p>
          Baby showers sit in a slightly different social space than birthday
          parties: guests often don't know each other well, the mood should stay
          warm rather than competitive, and the activities need to work across a
          wide range of ages and comfort levels. Spin wheels fit this well
          precisely because they're conversational, not confrontational — the
          wheel makes the call, and the group simply responds together.
        </p>

        <figure class="ai-image">
          <img
            src="/eng-emoji-picker-wheel.webp"
            alt="Soft pastel flat illustration of a lavender and mint baby shower spin wheel on a table decorated with tiny baby shoes, flowers, and a 'welcome baby' banner in the background"
