        <h3>The icebreaker wheel</h3>
        <p>
          The first twenty minutes of any baby shower tend to be a bit of social
          limbo — people arriving, finding seats, cautiously working out who
          they know. Running an icebreaker wheel from the start smooths this
          over nicely. Spin it whenever a new guest sits down, and they answer
          whatever segment lands — introductions happen naturally rather than
          feeling forced.
        </p>
        <p>Segment ideas for a baby shower icebreaker wheel:</p>
        <div class="pill-list">
          <span class="pill pill-purple">👶 How do you know the parents?</span>
          <span class="pill pill-pink">🔮 Predict the birth date</span>
          <span class="pill pill-teal"
            >📖 Share a piece of parenting advice</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-gold">🏆 Share your funniest baby story</span>
          <span class="pill pill-green"
            >🍼 Name a baby item you couldn't live without</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-purple"
            >💌 One word to describe the mum-to-be</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-pink"
            >🌟 Your best memory with the parents</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-teal">🎵 Suggest a lullaby for the baby</span>
        </div>

        <h3>The name-voting wheel</h3>
        <p>
          If the parents-to-be haven't settled on a name — or want to casually
          gather opinions without committing to anything — a name-voting wheel
          is a light, structured way to do it. Load the shortlist (real
          candidates, maybe a couple of joke names too) onto the wheel. Spin it
          for each guest; whoever it lands on has to argue for or against that
          name, and the room votes by applause. No pressure on the parents, and
          it produces genuinely useful, low-key input.
        </p>

        <h3>The prize game wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Baby shower prizes tend to skew softer and more personal than birthday
          prizes — pamper items, small keepsakes, or gift vouchers rather than
          toys. A few segment ideas:
        </p>
        <div class="pill-list">
          <span class="pill pill-pink">🕯️ Candle and bath set</span>
          <span class="pill pill-purple">💅 Nail polish duo</span>
          <span class="pill pill-teal">☕ Coffee shop gift card</span>
          <span class="pill pill-gold"
            >📸 Polaroid moment with the mum-to-be</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-green"
            >🌸 Flower of your choice from the centrepiece</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-pink">🎊 Spin again!</span>
          <span class="pill pill-purple">🍰 Pick your slice of cake first</span>
          <span class="pill pill-teal"
            >💌 Write a message in the baby book</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-gold">🥂 Lead the next toast</span>
        </div>

        <h3>The "advice for the parents" wheel</h3>
        <p>
          One of the more meaningful baby shower activities is gathering advice
          from guests — but open-ended roundtable sharing can feel slow and puts
          pressure on anyone who freezes up when asked to speak without a
          prompt. A wheel fixes this by handing each guest a specific,
          easy-to-answer question instead.
        </p>
        <p>Suggested advice prompt segments:</p>
        <div class="pill-list">
          <span class="pill pill-pink">😴 Best sleep advice</span>
          <span class="pill pill-purple">🍼 Feeding tip you swear by</span>
          <span class="pill pill-teal"
            >💑 How to keep your relationship strong</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-gold"
            >🤣 Funniest parenting mistake you made</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-green">📚 Book every parent should read</span>
          <span class="pill pill-pink">🌙 Surviving the first month</span>
          <span class="pill pill-purple"
            >🏡 What surprised you most about parenthood</span
          >
          <span class="pill pill-teal"
            >🎁 Most useful item no one thinks to buy</span
          >
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 6 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="gender-reveal">6. Gender Reveal Wheel Methods</h2>
        <div class="party-label purple">🍼 Baby Shower / Gender Reveal</div>

        <p>
          Gender reveal moments run on shared suspense and a collective reaction
          — which is exactly what a spin wheel is built to deliver. There are a
          few different ways to structure one, ranging from gentle to genuinely
          dramatic.
        </p>

        <h3>Method A: The celebratory reveal wheel</h3>
        <p>
          If the parents already want a theatrical group reveal (rather than
          finding out randomly alongside guests), you can build an alternating
          celebratory reveal wheel matching the known outcome across every
          segment — keeping blue and pink suspense vibes on screen while
          guaranteeing zero awkward misfires. On the other hand, if weighting is
          only shifted (`20x` vs `1x` with the <code>+</code> button), there is
          still roughly a ~4.8% chance of landing on the smaller slice!
        </p>

        <div class="box box-warn">
          <div class="box-label">⚠️ A note on transparency</div>
          <p>
            If you go with a weighted reveal wheel as above, know that the
            outcome isn't actually random — it's purely theatrical, and that's a
            valid choice for many families. If you'd rather the spin itself be
            the real reveal — with even the parents finding out live — build a
            genuine 50/50 wheel instead (coordinated in advance with whoever
            holds the sonographer's information). Either approach works; just
            decide which experience you want before you build the wheel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Method B: The mystery name-category reveal</h3>
        <p>
          Rather than a direct blue-or-pink reveal, load the wheel with a mix of
          names traditionally associated with each gender, without labeling
          which is which. The wheel lands on a name, and that name is the reveal
          — if the baby's a girl, the girl-associated names are the only ones
          actually loaded. This version feels warmer and more personal than a
          binary colour reveal, and it flows naturally into the parents talking
          about names.
        </p>

        <h3>Method C: The audience prediction wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Before revealing anything, spin a wheel of guest names and have
          whoever lands on it announce their prediction and their reasoning.
          Keep a visible tally — a whiteboard works well. Then do the reveal and
          see who called it, who was confidently wrong, and whose reasoning was
          the funniest. This turns a five-second moment into a fifteen-minute
          activity, and the shared guessing makes the reveal feel like something
          the whole room built together.
        </p>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 7 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="icebreaker">7. Icebreaker Wheels for Mixed Groups</h2>
        <div class="party-label teal">🌈 All Events</div>

        <p>
          Both birthday parties and baby showers regularly mix people who don't
          know one another — old friends and new partners, colleagues and
          childhood mates, relatives from different branches who haven't crossed
          paths in years. An icebreaker wheel solves the "first thirty minutes"
          problem by giving people something to talk about that isn't just "so
          how do you know the host?"
        </p>

        <p>
          The icebreaker wheels that work best across a mixed crowd share three
          traits: specific enough prompts to get an interesting answer,
          low-stakes enough that no one dreads being picked, and short enough to
          finish in under a minute.
        </p>

        <div class="idea-grid">
          <div class="idea-card teal">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🌍</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Format 01 · Universal</div>
            <h3>The Conversation Starter Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Prompts that open a conversation without demanding personal
              disclosure: "What's the most beautiful place you've been?",
              "What's the last thing that made you genuinely laugh?", "What
              skill do you wish you'd picked up earlier?", "What's your most
              unpopular food opinion?", "What would your own TV show be called?"
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🎭</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Format 02 · Party Energy</div>
            <h3>The "Two Truths and a Lie" Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Load the wheel with guest names. Whoever gets picked shares two
              truths and a lie, and the room gets 30 seconds to collectively
              vote on which is the lie before the reveal. It's one of the
              highest-energy formats because the vote itself is a shared
              activity, and the reveal is almost always a surprise.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card purple">
            <div class="idea-emoji">💬</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Format 03 · Baby Shower</div>
            <h3>The "Mum vs Dad" Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Fill the wheel with traits — "most patient", "most likely to
              handle night feeds", "better at silly voices", "will be stricter
              about screens", "better cook" — and let guests vote by pointing at
              mum or dad. Simple, warm, and reliably good for affectionate
              laughs, including from the parents-to-be themselves.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="idea-card gold">
            <div class="idea-emoji">🗓️</div>
            <div class="idea-num">Format 04 · Milestone Birthdays</div>
            <h3>The "Decade Memory" Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              For milestone birthdays (30, 40, 50+), load the wheel with eras —
              "age 10", "age 20", "the 90s", "first job years" — and whoever
              spins shares one memory from that period of the birthday person's
              life, or their own. This one tends to produce unexpectedly
              touching moments and a real reminder of how much shared history is
              in the room.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

