        <h2 id="setup">8. Building Your Wheel in Five Minutes</h2>

        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Open spinthewheelsonline.com on your party device</strong>
              <p>
                Head to <a href="/" class="ilink">spinthewheelsonline.com</a>
                on whatever screen will run the game — laptop, tablet, or phone.
                Connecting a laptop to a TV via HDMI gives the best visual
                result for living-room events, and most smart TVs can cast a
                phone screen directly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Type or paste your segment list</strong>
              <p>
                Add one label per line, kept short — four words or fewer reads
                best from a distance. For a prize wheel that's the prize name;
                for a dare wheel, a short prompt; for trivia, the topic. You can
                add anywhere from two to fifty-plus segments.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Adjust colours and sound to fit the vibe</strong>
              <p>
                Change each segment's colour, and toggle the spin sound on or
                off. For parties, keep the sound on — the ticking deceleration
                is a big part of what builds anticipation for each spin. If
                you're projecting, check the audio routes through your speakers
                before guests arrive.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Turn on "remove after spin" where repeats matter</strong>
              <p>
                For prize wheels where each prize should only go once, or name
                wheels calling each guest exactly one turn, enable
                remove-after-spin — winning segments disappear immediately. For
                dare wheels where repeats are fine, leave it off.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Save or bookmark the finished wheel</strong>
              <p>
                Your wheel's URL carries the full configuration. Bookmark it on
                any device you might use during the party. If you're running
                several wheels across the night (icebreaker → prize → dares),
                save each with a clear label so you can switch between them
                instantly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Do a full test spin before guests arrive</strong>
              <p>
                Run it once on the actual display you'll use. Check the text is
                legible at a normal viewing distance, the sound comes through,
                and fullscreen mode fills the screen properly. Thirty minutes of
                prep now means zero technical hiccups once the room's attention
                is on you.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

