        <h2 id="tips">10. Hosting Tips That Actually Matter</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Show the wheel to guests early.</strong> Right at the start
            of the party, mention it briefly — "we'll be using this throughout
            the night." Guests who see it early anticipate it as part of the
            event rather than being caught off guard by a surprise interruption
            later.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong
              >Cast to a TV or projector rather than passing a phone
              around.</strong
            >
            The gap between "a spin wheel on someone's phone" and "a spin wheel
            filling a 55-inch TV" is the gap between a private toy and a shared
            event. Any bigger screen you have access to is worth using — even a
            monitor propped on the dining table beats a phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Rotate who does the spinning.</strong> The room's energy
            shifts when the spinner isn't always the host. Let it rotate — each
            child takes a turn at a kids' party, or spin a separate name wheel
            at adult events to decide who spins the main wheel. It spreads the
            excitement and removes any sense that the host is controlling
            outcomes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Load your segments before guests arrive.</strong>
            Building a wheel while fifteen people watch is slow and a little
            awkward. Prepare everything the night before, test it on the actual
            party screen, and leave the tab open. The wheel should be ready the
            second the room needs it, not five minutes later.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Match segment count to how long the game will run.</strong>
            For a two-hour kids' party with roughly 15 minutes of wheel time,
            8–10 segments is about right — enough variety without repetition.
            For a full-afternoon baby shower running across several activities,
            12–15 segments gives you more than enough material.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep the sound on.</strong> The ticking during deceleration
            does a lot of the work in making a spin feel exciting rather than
            purely functional. Route the audio through decent speakers — a small
            Bluetooth speaker for garden or outdoor events makes a real
            difference. The moment of silence right before the reveal is usually
            where the room's energy peaks.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make sure every child gets a turn.</strong> If you're
            running a prize wheel for a group of children, aim for every child
            to interact with it at least once. Use a separate name wheel to call
            children up in order, then let each child spin the prize wheel once.
            It heads off the "I never got a go" complaints that can sour the end
            of a party.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Screenshot your finished segment list.</strong> If the
            browser crashes or the tab closes accidentally, having a screenshot
            means you can rebuild the wheel in under two minutes instead of
            starting over mid-party. Small bit of insurance, real peace of mind.
          </li>
        </ul>

