        <h2 id="faq">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What age group does a birthday party wheel work best for?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            It works across nearly every age, but the content on the wheel needs
            to match the crowd. For under-6s, keep every outcome physical and
            simple — nothing that requires reading or sitting still. For 7–12,
            light trivia and mild dares work well. Teens respond to social
            challenges and creative prompts. Adults tend to enjoy prize wheels
            and "how well do you know me?" trivia most. The mechanism suits
            everyone; only the content changes.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            How many segments should a party wheel have?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            8–12 segments tends to be the sweet spot for most parties. Fewer
            than 6 gets repetitive fast; more than 16 makes individual segments
            too small to read from across a room, and some outcomes end up
            rarely landing. If you've got a lot of content, build two or three
            separate wheels for different parts of the event rather than
            cramming it all into one.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can a spin wheel be used at an outdoor party?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — it runs in any browser, including on phones and tablets. The
            main things to plan for outdoors are screen visibility in daylight
            (direct sun can wash out a screen, so shade or a larger display
            helps) and audio (a portable Bluetooth speaker keeps the spin sound
            audible). Once the page has loaded, no ongoing internet connection
            is needed, so it works fine in a garden or park.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does a spin wheel work well when baby shower guests don't know each
            other?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            It's actually well suited to exactly that situation. The wheel
            removes the awkward "who goes first?" moment and spreads
            participation naturally. Start with a low-stakes icebreaker wheel
            rather than personal prompts — travel memories, food opinions,
            predictions about the baby — and comfort in the room builds quickly
            from there. Save more personal questions for later, once guests have
            warmed up a bit.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can I match the wheel's colours to my party theme?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — spinthewheelsonline.com lets you set an individual colour for
            every segment. For a pink-and-gold baby shower, alternate those two
            colours across segments. For a superhero birthday, use the
            character's palette. For a gender reveal in blue and pink, use
            exactly those two. Matching the wheel to your decorations makes it
            feel like a planned part of the event rather than an afterthought.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What if the same dare or question comes up twice?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Turn on "remove after spin," which permanently takes each segment
            off the wheel once it lands — guaranteeing every outcome appears
            exactly once. For dare wheels where a repeat is fine (it'll land on
            a different person anyway), you can leave it off. For trivia or
            conversation wheels where a repeated question would feel stale,
            remove-after-spin is the better setting.
          </div>
        </div>


